Task 1: Creating and Editing Files with nano
Subtask 1.1: Launching nano and Creating a File
Open your terminal and run:
nano first_file.txt
Observe the interface:
Bottom bar shows key commands (^ = Ctrl)
Blinking cursor indicates insert mode
Subtask 1.2: Basic Text Operations
Type sample text:

Hello OpenShift!
This is my first text file.
Navigation:

Use arrow keys to move cursor
Ctrl+A: Move to line start
Ctrl+E: Move to line end
Save the file:

Ctrl+O (Write Out)
Press Enter to confirm filename
Ctrl+X to exit
Expected Outcome: File first_file.txt created in current directory with your text.

Task 2: Advanced Editing with vi
Subtask 2.1: Entering vi and Basic Modes
Create a new file:
vi second_file.txt
Understand modes:
Command mode (default on launch)
Insert mode (press i to enter)
Visual mode (press v)
Subtask 2.2: Editing Operations
Enter insert mode (i) and add:

Podman Container Basics:
1. Images
2. Containers
3. Pods
Return to command mode (Esc)

Delete line 2:

Navigate to line 2
Type dd
Save and exit:

:wq then Enter
Troubleshooting Tip: If stuck in insert mode, press Esc multiple times to return to command mode.

Task 3: File Manipulation Commands
Subtask 3.1: Viewing File Contents
Use cat:
cat first_file.txt
Paginated view with less:
less second_file.txt
(Press q to quit)
Subtask 3.2: File Encoding and Line Endings
Check file encoding:

file first_file.txt
(Typically shows "ASCII text")

Convert line endings (LF ↔ CRLF):

# Convert to DOS format
unix2dos first_file.txt

# Convert back to Unix
dos2unix first_file.txt
Key Concept: Unix uses LF (\n), Windows uses CRLF (\r\n)

Task 4: Practical Application
Subtask 4.1: Create a Podman Configuration Snippet
Create new file:
nano podman_config.conf
Add content:
[storage]
driver = "overlay"
runroot = "/var/run/containers/storage"
graphroot = "/var/lib/containers/storage"
Subtask 4.2: Validate File Integrity
Check for trailing whitespace:
grep -n '[[:space:]]$' podman_config.conf
Count lines:
wc -l podman_config.conf
